## Executive Summary

This paper examines the central commercial requirement now confronting frontier artificial-intelligence companies:

> Having built an increasingly capable machine, they must now cause an Amazon-sized revenue engine to emerge from it before the infrastructure commitments mature, prices collapse, competitors catch up, suppliers stop financing the show, or the audience notices that intelligence and profit are not the same physical quantity.

The Institute designates this problem:

**AER — the Amazon Extraction Requirement.**

AER occurs when a research system is expected to generate not merely a useful product, but an entire vertically integrated economic order containing subscriptions, enterprise software, advertising, commerce, developer platforms, marketplaces, agents, scientific licensing, robotics, consumer hardware and recurring high-margin revenue.

The hat currently contains a language model.

The investors require Amazon, AWS, Google Ads, Microsoft Office, an app store, a robot economy and preferably a modest cure for cancer to come out when it is shaken.

OpenAI’s own published figures show both available compute and annualised revenue rising approximately threefold each year from 2023 to 2025. Compute rose from 0.2 GW to about 1.9 GW; annualised revenue rose from $2 billion to more than $20 billion.[1] This establishes extraordinary demand. It does not yet establish operating leverage. If revenue and the scarce physical input required to produce it climb together, scale may be enlarging the machine without separating income from cost.

The financial scale is already industrial. OpenAI announced in March 2026 that it had closed a round containing $122 billion in committed capital at an $852 billion post-money valuation.[2] Reuters later reported that the company was targeting roughly $600 billion in total compute spending through 2030, while The Information reported estimated compute-spending commitments of $665 billion through the same period.[3][4] These figures are not one conventional debt instrument payable on a single afternoon. They mix planned spending, capacity contracts, supplier agreements and financing structures. The legal form varies. The requirement to produce enough economic value to support the system does not.

The same pattern appears elsewhere. Anthropic was reported to have committed $200 billion over five years to Google Cloud and more than $100 billion over ten years to Amazon’s cloud technologies.[5][6] xAI’s physical buildout has already produced a less theoretical indicator: in July 2026, a contractor filed liens alleging more than $136 million remained unpaid for work on Colossus and Colossus II, with further threatened claims potentially taking the contractor’s asserted total above half a billion dollars. SpaceXAI did not respond to the broadcaster’s request for comment, and the claims had not been judicially determined at the time of reporting.[7]

Meanwhile, part of the sector’s market value is being generated before the underlying business operates. The US Securities and Exchange Commission has brought multiple “AI washing” cases involving false or misleading claims about AI capabilities.[8][9] In April 2026, footwear company Allbirds announced that it would sell its shoe assets, rename the remaining company NewBird AI and attempt to become a GPU-as-a-Service provider. Its shares rose 582% in one day.[10][11]

The adjective had begun producing value before the servers did.

The robotics escape route is also narrower than presentations imply. Large clusters can train models, aggregate fleet data, run simulation, perform non-time-critical planning and distribute updates. They cannot safely serve as the sole real-time control loop for machines moving around people through an ordinary wide-area network. Safety-related control systems must be designed around defined functions, failure behaviour and continuous operation; real-time robotics requires bounded deadlines, while network latency and jitter can destabilise continuous control and produce unsafe execution.[12][13][14]

The cluster may be the university.

It cannot be the spinal cord.

A robot approaching a pedestrian cannot display:

> Reconnecting to Virginia…

The principal conclusion is that the path from capability to profit contains far more independent steps than the current financing narrative admits.

Intelligence can create options.

Profit requires ownership, distribution, pricing power, execution, cost control and a mechanism for capturing part of the value created.

The rabbit is not AGI.

The rabbit is margin.

