## 1. Statement of the Trick

The public argument is commonly presented as:

\[
\text{More intelligence}
\Rightarrow
\text{more value}
\Rightarrow
\text{more revenue}
\Rightarrow
\text{profit}
\]

Only the first implication is even directionally automatic.

A more capable system may create more useful outputs. That does not determine:

* who owns the resulting invention;
* whether the customer can reproduce it with a cheaper model;
* whether the output is legally protectable;
* whether the customer is willing to pay more than the cost of generation;
* whether the model provider captures the value or merely supplies a low-margin input;
* whether the application requires expensive human checking;
* or whether a rival gives away an equivalent capability to sell something else.

The corrected relationship is:

\[
\text{Economic capture}
=
C \times A \times W \times \alpha
\]

where:

* \(C\) = useful capability;
* \(A\) = adoption;
* \(W\) = willingness to pay;
* \(\alpha\) = the provider’s capture fraction.

Profit then becomes:

\[
\Pi
=
C A W \alpha
-
(C_{\mathrm{inference}}
+
C_{\mathrm{training}}
+
C_{\mathrm{R\&D}}
+
C_{\mathrm{distribution}}
+
C_{\mathrm{capital}}
+
C_{\mathrm{failure}})
\]

A frontier model can improve \(C\).

It does not automatically maximize \(A\), preserve \(W\), own \(\alpha\), or reduce any cost term.

This gap is designated:

**CCG — the Capability–Capture Gap.**

The CCG explains why a model can generate something economically valuable without the model company receiving the value. A drug candidate may create value for a pharmaceutical company. A factory optimisation may create value for a manufacturer. A software design may create value for the user who deploys it. Unless the model provider owns the downstream asset, charges outcome-linked fees, controls distribution or retains a marketplace toll, the provider may receive only the price of the tokens used to produce the answer.

The intelligence may discover the gold.

The contract decides who owns the mine.

