## 2. The Hat Is Already Hundreds of Billions of Dollars Deep

OpenAI’s official commercial narrative is unusually clear.

The company says it operates consumer subscriptions, workplace subscriptions, usage-priced APIs, commerce and advertising. It describes a flywheel:

\[
\text{Compute}
\rightarrow
\text{capability}
\rightarrow
\text{products}
\rightarrow
\text{adoption}
\rightarrow
\text{revenue}
\rightarrow
\text{more compute}
\]

It reported the following relationship:[1]

| Year | Available compute | Annualised revenue |
|---|---:|---:|
| 2023 | 0.2 GW | $2B |
| 2024 | 0.6 GW | $6B |
| 2025 | ~1.9 GW | $20B+ |

This is a remarkable growth curve.

It is also almost a diagram of the problem.

Compute grew approximately 9.5 times.

Annualised revenue grew approximately 10 times.

The company interprets this as evidence that more compute would have produced more adoption and monetisation. That may be correct. But the same figures do not demonstrate that each additional dollar of revenue requires proportionally less infrastructure, research or delivery cost.

The flywheel currently reads:

\[
\text{More compute}
\rightarrow
\text{more revenue}
\rightarrow
\text{requirement for still more compute}
\]

A flywheel is commercially useful only when some part of each rotation remains as cash.

Otherwise it is a beautifully engineered method for moving money from investors to electricity suppliers at increasing angular velocity.

The distinction between annualised run-rate revenue and recognised yearly revenue also matters. OpenAI reported a $20 billion-plus annualised rate for 2025, while Reuters reported $13 billion of actual 2025 revenue.[1][3] Neither figure is deceptive; they measure different things. But the larger run-rate number is the one naturally selected for the stage presentation, while the annual cost base continues arriving in calendar time.

The company then closed $122 billion in committed capital.[2]

At this point the conventional startup metaphor becomes inadequate.

This is not a small boat crossing a cash-flow gap.

It is a private industrial mobilisation attempting to construct the bridge, the river, the electricity market and the future customers simultaneously.

