## 3. The Sixfold Revenue Levitation

Suppose a business must multiply revenue sixfold within three years.

The required compound annual growth rate is:

\[
g = 6^{1/3}-1 \approx 81.7\%
\]

Thus:

\[
R_{t+1} \approx 1.817R_t
\]

every year, for three consecutive years.

Starting from a $24 billion annualised rate, sixfold growth produces approximately:

\[
R_3 = \$144\text{ billion}
\]

That sounds enormous because it is.

But turnover is not repayment capacity.

At a 33% gross margin:

\[
\$144\text{B} \times 0.33 = \$47.52\text{B}
\]

At a 39% gross margin:

\[
\$144\text{B} \times 0.39 = \$56.16\text{B}
\]

Those sums are gross profit before research and development, sales, administration, financing, fresh training runs, product failures, legal costs and whatever a gigawatt-scale buildout eventually does to a procurement department.

The Information reported that OpenAI’s first-quarter 2026 revenue was $5.7 billion, cash burn was $3.7 billion, cost of revenue was $3.5 billion, R&D spending was $8.6 billion and operating loss was $9.3 billion. It reported a 39% gross margin, up from 33% a year earlier. Reuters said it could not independently verify the report.[4][15]

The improvement from 33% to 39% is meaningful.

It is not the same as profitability.

It means the rabbit has developed a healthier coat while still eating the theatre.

For a normal software company, rapid revenue growth often spreads fixed development costs across a much larger customer base. Frontier AI contains a more difficult mixture:

* large fixed research costs;
* large recurring training costs;
* substantial variable inference costs;
* capacity purchased years in advance;
* competitive pressure to lower prices;
* user demand for larger context windows and deeper reasoning;
* and model improvements that can make each successful request more computationally expensive.

The commercial trick is therefore not simply:

> Sell more intelligence.

It is:

> Sell dramatically more intelligence while the cost of producing each useful unit falls faster than market prices, competitors remain expensive enough to preserve margin, and customers do not decide that last quarter’s open model is sufficient.

This is classified as:

**MCL — the Margin Compression Levitation.**

The object must rise while gravity increases.

