## 4. Revenue and Compute Are Holding Hands

OpenAI’s own revenue–compute chart is one of the most important disclosures in the entire industry because it shows the current dependency directly.

The system is growing.

The system is not yet evidently decoupling.

For operating leverage, one wants something like:

\[
\frac{\Delta R}{\Delta C_{\mathrm{compute}}}
\uparrow
\]

or, more practically:

\[
\text{revenue per delivered unit of compute}
\uparrow
\]

while the all-in cost of that compute falls.

Instead, the reported 2023–2025 curves move almost together.[1]

This may change. Better chips, quantisation, distillation, scheduling, caching, model routing and specialised inference hardware can reduce cost. OpenAI explicitly says it is moving high-volume workloads to lower-cost infrastructure and delivering some useful intelligence at costs measured in cents per million tokens.[1]

But efficiency contains its own commercial trap.

If all providers receive similar hardware and algorithmic gains, market prices may fall alongside costs.

Thus:

\[
\text{Cost reduction}
\not\Rightarrow
\text{margin expansion}
\]

unless:

\[
\text{Cost reduction}
>
\text{price reduction}
\]

and the provider retains enough differentiation to prevent immediate commoditisation.

This is why “intelligence will become too cheap to meter” and “this will be an extraordinarily profitable metered business” are not naturally compatible statements.

If intelligence becomes a commodity, the durable profits may accrue to:

* chip designers;
* power generators;
* data-centre owners;
* network operators;
* application companies with customer relationships;
* marketplaces;
* regulated domain specialists;
* or whoever owns the expensive physical thing the intelligence improves.

The model provider risks becoming the cleverest wholesale ingredient in somebody else’s profitable sandwich.

The Institute’s earlier finding remains operationally relevant:

> Possessing the file does not mean possessing the factory.

Open weights can lower the price of model capability while increasing demand for chips, memory, networking and power. The intelligence layer becomes more open. The silicon tollbooth remains firmly staffed.

