## 5. The Adjective Has Acquired a Market Capitalisation

The Institute now recognises a new intangible asset:

**AIAP — the Artificial Intelligence Adjective Premium.**

AIAP is the increase in perceived enterprise value obtained by placing “AI” near an existing product, strategy, fund, dashboard, toothbrush or distressed corporate shell.

The phenomenon is not purely satirical.

In March 2024, the US Securities and Exchange Commission charged two investment advisers over false and misleading statements about their use of AI. The firms agreed to pay $400,000 in civil penalties.[8]

In October 2024, the SEC charged Rimar Capital entities and individuals over claims involving a purported AI-driven trading platform. The SEC said nearly $4 million had been raised from 45 investors and described the conduct as “AI washing.”[9]

In June 2024, the SEC charged the founder of AI recruitment startup Joonko with allegedly defrauding investors of at least $21 million through false claims about customers, candidates and revenue. The SEC called it old-school fraud using new-school buzzwords.[16]

A 2022 study of AI-named exchange-traded funds found that their underlying constituent stocks experienced approximately 0.4% more cumulative abnormal returns around ETF inception than constituents of comparable funds without AI in the name.[17]

Then the market performed the control experiment itself.

In April 2026, Allbirds announced that, after agreeing to sell its footwear assets, the remaining company would pursue AI compute infrastructure, seek the name NewBird AI and use a $50 million financing facility to acquire GPUs.[10] Reuters reported that the shares rose 582% in one day.[11]

The company had changed from shoes to servers in a press release.

The market changed its value before the first rack was warm.

The useful finding is narrower and measurable:

\[
\text{Narrative value}
\neq
\text{operating cash flow}
\]

For a period, however:

\[
\text{Narrative value}
\rightarrow
\text{access to capital}
\]

and access to capital can purchase enough infrastructure to keep the narrative alive.

The adjective is therefore not merely marketing.

It is temporary collateral.

