## 6. The Rabbit’s Suppliers Are Financing the Performance

The AI infrastructure market contains an unusual degree of overlap between:

* investor;
* supplier;
* customer;
* landlord;
* chip vendor;
* cloud provider;
* and valuation beneficiary.

OpenAI’s February 2026 funding announcement included $50 billion from Amazon and $30 billion from NVIDIA alongside strategic arrangements involving Amazon cloud services and dedicated NVIDIA systems.[18]

Anthropic was reported to have committed $200 billion to Google Cloud over five years and more than $100 billion to Amazon cloud technologies over ten years.[5][6] Reuters Breakingviews later described Amazon as making up to $20 billion of financing available for additional Anthropic equity while Anthropic remained a major AWS customer.[19]

This structure is not automatically improper.

Strategic suppliers have financed customers throughout industrial history.

It does, however, complicate the apparent direction of demand.

A supplier invests in the model company.

The model company uses capital to purchase the supplier’s infrastructure.

The supplier records demand and gains an equity stake whose valuation rises as the model company raises again.

The model company cites secured infrastructure and investor confidence as evidence of future scale.

The next investor observes supplier demand and valuation growth.

The rabbit is passed around the circle until everyone has briefly recorded possession of it.

We designate this:

**VCFL — the Vendor–Customer Financing Loop.**

Its simplified form is:

\[
S \rightarrow L \rightarrow S
\]

where \(S\) is supplier capital and \(L\) is lab expenditure.

The commercial question is not whether the circle exists.

It is whether a sufficiently large flow of money eventually enters from unrelated, paying end customers and remains after the suppliers, financiers and infrastructure owners take their shares.

Without that external flow, the system is not a flywheel.

It is a roundabout with accounting.

The contractor claims around xAI provide a useful physical reminder. Action News 5 reported that Darana Hybrid filed two liens alleging more than $136 million remained unpaid for construction work on Colossus sites, while two other companies filed smaller claims. The main contractor said further Mississippi filings could raise its asserted total above $500 million. SpaceXAI did not respond to the outlet’s request for comment.[7]

The legal merits remain to be resolved.

The industrial lesson does not require a verdict:

> Someone must eventually pay the people who poured the concrete.

The cloud may be virtual.

The invoice is extremely embodied.

