## 7. The Robot Escape Hatch Meets the Speed of Light

Robotics is frequently presented as the final answer to AI monetisation:

1. train a general model;
2. place it in millions of machines;
3. charge for intelligence continuously;
4. obtain the robot economy;
5. locate profitability beneath the sofa.

The first problem is physical control.

A machine interacting with people contains several different computational layers:

| Layer | Appropriate location |
|---|---|
| Model training and fleet learning | Large cluster |
| Simulation and long-horizon optimisation | Cluster or regional edge |
| Non-urgent mission planning | Cloud, edge or onboard |
| Perception and local world state | Onboard or tightly bounded edge |
| Motion control | Local deterministic controller |
| Safety functions and emergency stop | Local safety-rated system |
| Safe state after communication loss | Local and immediate |

The cluster can improve the robot.

It cannot be consulted for permission every time the wheel must stop.

ISO 13849-1:2023 specifies methodology and requirements for safety-related parts of control systems performing safety functions in high-demand and continuous modes, including software.[12] It does not prescribe one universal architecture or contain the sentence “thou shalt not use a cloud.” It requires designers to establish and validate safety functions and performance appropriate to the machinery.

That is already enough to make the following safety case unattractive:

> Braking response is normally excellent, except during congestion, route failure, cloud maintenance, DNS problems, account suspension, fibre damage, scheduler overload, regional outage or the moment somebody drives the machine into a lift.

ROS 2’s real-time documentation explains that robotics control loops must meet periodic deadlines with tightly bounded jitter. In its 1 kHz example, a maximum latency that exceeds the one-millisecond update period is described as unacceptable because the controller has missed the loop entirely.[13]

Research on safety-critical edge robotics therefore focuses on upper-bounding end-to-end latency and predictable fault-tolerant execution near the robot.[14] A 2026 cloud-robotics paper states the problem more directly: network latency and jitter can destabilise continuous manipulation, cause command starvation and produce unsafe physical execution. Its proposed solution keeps verification and buffered execution at the local edge rather than trusting each movement to a remote round trip.[20]

The practical architecture is hybrid:

\[
\text{Cloud intelligence}
+
\text{edge autonomy}
+
\text{local safety}
\]

not:

\[
\text{Cloud intelligence}
=
\text{remote nervous system}
\]

The emergency stop may not be a subscription feature.

The machine cannot require a fresh API response before deciding not to run over a person.

No insurer wishes to receive the sentence:

> The server in Virginia was thinking.

This matters economically because robotics does not automatically convert every physical action into centrally metered cluster revenue. Each serious robot requires its own local processors, sensors, controllers, redundancy, validation, maintenance and safe failure behaviour.

The frontier lab may sell:

* models;
* training;
* fleet-learning services;
* high-level planning;
* simulation;
* updates;
* specialised accelerators;
* or licensing.

But part of the intelligence stack necessarily migrates towards the machine.

The robot is not a thin client with legs.

It is an expensive distributed system that can fall on somebody.

