## 8. The Labour-Saving Rabbit Has a Maintenance Department

The enterprise sales case frequently assumes:

\[
\text{Tasks automated}
\times
\text{salary per worker}
=
\text{value available to the model provider}
\]

This is the spreadsheet form of Fully Automated Delusionalism.

It omits the work created around the automation.

A deployed AI system generates a shadow workload:

* integration;
* permissions;
* data cleaning;
* evaluation;
* exception handling;
* cybersecurity;
* audit;
* supervision;
* process redesign;
* staff training;
* incident response;
* and the recurring meeting in which someone asks why the autonomous agent emailed the entire customer database.

The corrected equation is:

\[
\text{Net labour saving}
=
L_{\mathrm{visible\ removed}}
-
L_{\mathrm{stabilisation}}
-
L_{\mathrm{verification}}
-
L_{\mathrm{exceptions}}
-
L_{\mathrm{governance}}
-
L_{\mathrm{repair}}
\]

The saving may remain large.

It is not equal to all visible work removed.

This matters twice.

First, the model provider cannot automatically charge the customer the full nominal wage value of every task touched by AI. The customer must retain part of the gain to justify adoption and must also pay for the new stabilisation layer.

Second, deleting beginner work can delete the route by which experienced operators are produced.

The Institute’s earlier Training Laser finding applies directly:

> You cannot have experts without apprenticeships, and you cannot have apprenticeships without mistakes on low-stakes work.

If AI removes drafting, basic analysis, routine coding, first-line diagnosis and junior production work, organisations may obtain immediate throughput while weakening the training gradient that produces future reviewers, engineers, clinicians and supervisors.

The difficult work does not disappear.

It becomes concentrated at the top of the system among fewer people who had less opportunity to learn it.

This is the:

**ASD — Automation Supervision Deficit.**

The company then attempts to solve ASD by hiring experienced staff from a labour market whose entry routes it has just automated.

The revenue presentation counts the removed junior role.

The cost model quietly omits the future expert who no longer exists.

This is commercially relevant because a product sold on labour replacement must survive the full system cost of:

\[
\text{automation}
+
\text{human residuals}
+
\text{competence preservation}
\]

A model can accelerate the worker.

It does not remove the factory’s need for somebody who understands what the answer is supposed to look like.

