## 11. Scenario Analysis

### 11.1 Scenario A — The Rabbit Is Real

Inference costs collapse faster than prices.

Enterprise adoption expands.

OpenAI or another lab becomes a dominant operating layer for knowledge work.

Advertising, commerce, APIs, subscriptions and marketplaces compound.

Research costs become a declining proportion of revenue.

Capacity is matched successfully to demand.

Under this scenario, the industry has created a new platform category and the apparent madness becomes one of history’s great capital-allocation victories.

Required condition:

\[
\Delta \text{customer value}
>
\Delta \text{all-in cost}
\]

at enormous scale.

### 11.2 Scenario B — Permanent Fundraising Becomes the Product

Revenue continues rising rapidly.

Costs rise with it.

Each round finances the capacity required to justify the next valuation.

The company remains operationally important but structurally dependent on new equity, debt, supplier finance or public-market capital.

The rabbit remains visible.

It is never owned outright.

### 11.3 Scenario C — The Suppliers Win

Model capability becomes competitive and increasingly interchangeable.

Cloud providers, chip vendors, data-centre owners and power suppliers retain the scarce physical inputs.

Application companies own the customers.

The frontier labs discover that they created enormous economic value while occupying the narrowest-margin layer.

This is the **Wholesale Genius Outcome**.

Humanity receives intelligence.

NVIDIA receives gross margin.

### 11.4 Scenario D — Infrastructure Rationalisation

Demand fails to meet contracted capacity.

Projects are delayed.

Contracts are renegotiated.

Assets change owners.

Equity holders are diluted.

Special-purpose vehicles absorb losses.

The surviving infrastructure remains useful, but the original ownership structure does not.

The data centre continues humming.

The cap table becomes a historical document.

### 11.5 Scenario E — The Boring Businesses Capture the Revolution

Manufacturers, banks, hospitals, game platforms, logistics firms and software vendors adopt cheaper models inside existing profitable operations.

They possess the domain expertise, customers, regulatory permissions and distribution.

AI improves their margins.

The foundational model becomes a competitive input rather than the final economic sovereign.

The most commercially successful AI company may therefore be a company whose customers never describe it as an AI company.

This would be deeply upsetting to the adjective.

