## 14. Final Determination

The frontier AI companies have built systems of genuine and increasing usefulness.

They have also attached those systems to capital requirements so large that ordinary product success may be insufficient.

The commercial target is no longer:

> Build a popular software service.

It is:

> Convert a research breakthrough into a universal platform, preserve technical leadership, reduce unit cost faster than price, secure sovereign-scale infrastructure, invent multiple new markets, survive regulation, distribute intelligence into physical machines, and retain enough of the downstream value to pay everyone involved.

That is not impossible.

It is simply an unusually elaborate definition of “the business model will emerge.”

OpenAI’s own disclosures identify the pieces: subscriptions, APIs, agents, commerce, advertising, licensing, intellectual-property agreements and outcome pricing.[1] These are plausible revenue mechanisms.

They are not automatic consequences of intelligence.

Each requires construction, sales, contracts, trust, distribution and cost discipline.

The robot market does not remove this problem because the machine still needs local control, local safety and local compute.

The scientific market does not remove it because discovery must be validated and ownership negotiated.

The advertising market does not remove it because trust can be spent only once.

The enterprise market does not remove it because every “autonomous coworker” arrives with integration, governance and liability.

The capital market can postpone the problem.

It cannot convert expenditure into margin by declaration.

The sector’s central task is therefore accurately described as commercial magic, not because it violates physics, but because the number of independent conditions that must align resembles a stage illusion in which every trapdoor, mirror, cable, assistant, spotlight and audience member must work perfectly at once.

The audience has already paid for the theatre.

The suppliers have financed the hat.

The contractors have built the stage.

The rabbit has been valued at $852 billion.

Management must now reveal that it is, in fact, Amazon.

